A||||11||Phantom Rage||Steering, ¾ inch Play?||||5||
Z||000000||Phantom Rage||02-06-2001||05:41 PM||||Steering, ¾ inch Play?<P>Since purchasing my 88 coupe, I have always noticed about ¾ inch play in the steering wheel.  I have recently rebuilt part of the front end with new, upper and lower ball joints, outer tie rod ends, polys, lowering springs, and shocks.  The ¾ play still remains.  <P>I think the inner tie rods are still in good shape, since neither rack bellows (boots) are not torn, but I am not completely sure though.  How can I find out?<P>Also, Rodney sells an aluminum 88-rack bushing repair kit.  How can I find out if my bushing is bad as well?<P>Does anyone have suggestions?  <P>Please help Thank You!<P><BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bob Englert||02-06-2001||06:01 PM||||I think you need to get under the car while someone rocks the wheel back and forth and look for where the play is in the steering.  If the inner tie rods are not moving at all when the wheel is rocked through the play area, then I would suspect the rack itself is worn.  You might be able to increase the rack pinion preload a little to try to tighten it up, but it might be time for a replacement rack from the Fiero Store.  <P>Also I would grab each inner tie rod and see if there is any play between the inner tie rod and the rack itself.  If a inner tie rod needs replacing, Rodney Dickman has the best deal on new 88 inner tie rods.<P>Basically, you need to see where the play is occuring.<P>Good luck...<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phantom Rage||02-06-2001||08:07 PM||||Thanks Bob I’ll try that.  I was really hoping that after the suspension upgrade that everything would be ok.   I’m just ticked that I will have to pay another $50 to get her aligned again.   <BR>    <BR>||||reg||7
Z||000003||Alex4mula||02-07-2001||09:11 AM||||Hey Bob;<BR>How do you increase the rack pinion preload??<BR>I have like a 1/2" play & I have replaced all my suspension trying to eliminate it. Finally I went under there and notice that the play is in the rack itself. I would like to try that preload adjustment before buying a new rack. What annoys me more is that my car darts from side to side on any road groove due to that. I will replace my rims soon so I need to fix it. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||02-07-2001||03:25 PM||||If you can look at the top of the rack, where the steering shaft goes in, there is a big nut (I believe) with a locknut on it. Preload can be adjusted by loosening the locknut, and tightening the other (center) nut. Before you change anything, it might pay to mark the location of the nut and locknut so that you can return to where you are, if the changes don't do any good. <BR>I found that when I had the play adjusted out, the rack would bind when I was off center, so I had to loosen it up again. <BR>(This is in my 88 coupe. I have another rack that I'm going to put in, when I do the motor.)<BR>BTW... I found that the easiest way to get to the adjustment was to remove the plastic trunk liner. Just took a few minutes.<BR>Also, you won't have to have the entire thing realigned, even if you replace the rack. All you will have to do is set the toe-in. You can do that yourself.   <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||02-07-2001||04:39 PM||||Thanks Raydar!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||02-07-2001||04:39 PM||||Do you mean rotational play? E.g. when you turn the wheel the rim moves 3/4" before the car reacts.<P>Or do you have linear play? E.g. you can move the wheel up/down, left/right, or diagonally by 3/4"<P>Adjusting rack preload is covererd in the shop manual (which you have, right???). When adjusting rack preload, remove the adjuster plug, drill a small hole in it and install a Zerk fitting. Once you reinstall it, pump a hundred squirts or more of your favorite grease into the rack as you helper turns it back and forth from lock to lock. Most high mileage racks are in desparate need of lubrication.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex4mula||02-07-2001||04:52 PM||||In my case is rotational play. My rack has two fittings to add grease. I bought it from Fiero Store 2 years ago and has no more than 12K miles. Maybe the rebiuld was not done right....||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phantom Rage||02-07-2001||04:53 PM||||I love this forum!  I'm sure glad you asked Alex4mula, I forgot to.  I am also glad that Radar replied.  I hope that it works, wooohoooo!  If not, it is still good to hear that she will not have to be realigned.<P>Rotational play Will. Yes, have shop manual.  Also good advice, I will have to run down to the junkyard for some zerk fittings.  <P>P.S.  Does any know if the rack for the 88 Formula or GT have a different ratio than the standard 88 coupe?  My Brothers Formula always felt quicker, but mine seemed to have reduced effort (both with the same tires, size, and psi.)<P>Thanks<P><p>[This message has been edited by Phantom Rage (edited 02-07-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000009||Bob Englert||02-07-2001||05:33 PM||||For most years except the 84's the coupes and SE's had 3 1/2 turn racks, while the GT's stayed with the 3 turn rack that was standard in 84.  The 3 1/2 turn rack will have a little less steering effort than the faster 3 turn rack.<P>Tire size and inflation pressure can also have a significant effect on steering effort.  Also the redesigned suspension on the 88's resulted in less steering effort than the pre-88's.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||02-07-2001||06:28 PM||||Haven't counted turns lock-to-lock between my coupe and my Formula, but the Fiero Store only lists one part# for the 88. <p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 02-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||02-07-2001||06:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>When adjusting rack preload, remove the adjuster plug, drill a small hole in it and install a Zerk fitting. Once you reinstall it, pump a hundred squirts or more of your favorite grease into the rack as you helper turns it back and forth from lock to lock. Most high mileage racks are in desparate need of lubrication.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Great suggestion, Will! I'll have to try that, too.||||reg||1
